cluster: route the GLM lanes via a recorded operator override (v0.85)

The AIDER campaign returned UNRESOLVED for GLM and the best sample it could reach put
GLM behind the best deepseek lane: GLM combined 0.271 (n=299) against
deepseek-v4.1-flash 0.414 (n=133), and kimi-k3 ahead of both at 0.624 (n=101) but
priced at $3.00/$12.50 per 1k. The product owner has directed that the GLM lanes be
routed anyway, on price and category judgment: glm-5.3-flash is the $0.15/$0.50 lane
and the strongest model available at that price.

v0.85 = v0.83 geometry (centroids.bin, model_axes.json, model_registry.json,
model_features.json byte-identical) plus an explicit additive bias on the two GLM
lanes' cells in quality_means.json, which is what blendScoresV2 actually reads at
runtime. Every biased cell keeps its measured value under
meta.operator_override.measured_cells_preserved, with the rationale and the reversal
instructions carried inside the artifact, so the evidence surface is not overwritten
and the override stays attributable.

Measured on the 180-probe register corpus through production defaults:

  glm-5.3-flash  11 (6.1%) -> 44 (24.4%)
  glm-5.3         0        -> 4  (2.2%)
  models reached 7/8       -> 8/8

with TestRegisterProbeMixDoesNotCollapse still passing (floor 4 distinct winners) and
every other lane still taking traffic.

Two things learned while building it, both recorded because they are the traps the next
person hits:

1. Overriding rankings.json does nothing. It is the v1 legacy path; v2 bundles blend
   from quality_means.json.
2. A raw additive offset applied before the runtime's per-cluster min-max
   normalization flattens the whole cell range and collapses routing onto the
   preferred lanes - 2 of 8 models reached. Expressed instead as a fraction of each
   cluster's own spread (the current 0.25), the mix stays open. The builder takes the
   offset in range-relative terms for exactly this reason and fails loudly if it
   biases nothing.

kimi-k3 is deliberately not preferred despite leading the measurement, because its
per-token cost rules it out for general traffic. The corpus stays intact so this choice
can be revisited when new evidence lands.
